The pre-push hook (hooks/pre-push) calls `git push "$REMOTE" refs/notes/issue-*` at line 38 to sync issue notes. This git push itself triggers the pre-push hook again, causing recursive invocations. Each invocation prints 'Pushing N issue notes to origin...' and spawns another git push, resulting in hundreds of duplicate log lines and significant delay.

Observed behavior: pushing to origin with 99 issue notes produces 300+ lines of 'Pushing 99 issue notes to origin...' before eventually completing. The push takes much longer than expected.

The hook has no recursion guard. The recursion eventually terminates because the nested push succeeds (notes are already up-to-date on the second call), but the output and delay are problematic.

Suggested fix: add a re-entrancy guard using an environment variable:

```bash
# Prevent recursive invocation
if [[ -n "$GIT_ISSUE_PUSHING_NOTES" ]]; then
    exit 0
fi
export GIT_ISSUE_PUSHING_NOTES=1
```

Add this near the top of the hook, before the sync logic.
